    </files>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="prescriptive">
    <step order="0">Branch (constraint 1); declare FULL rigor; /conflict-check for CallerWriteAuthorization.cs and the three solution files.</step>
    <step order="1">Design (notes/task-044-notes.md): the profile table (column x profile x read/create/update), the identities in each writer profile on dev (read-only MCP query of fieldsecurityprofile membership and systemuser.applicationid), and which identity each server writer of the pair uses (044(c)).</step>
    <step order="2">Grep proof (044, UAC-r2): every Xrm.WebApi / Web API / form-script write in src/client/** and src/solutions/** (excluding node_modules and dist) checked for sprk_corerecordtype and sprk_corerecordid; record the command and the result.</step>
    <step order="3">Send the design and the Grep proof to UAC-r2 and the profile change to ontology-r1; record both answers. Stop here until both agree.</step>
    <step order="4">Write AC3-AC5 tests failing first; change the two Entity.xml files, FieldSecurityProfiles.xml and CallerWriteAuthorization step 3.</step>
    <step order="5">Build; verify-suites.sh and ArchTests; publish delta; CVE. Two adversarial passes (security tag); fix every finding.</step>
    <step order="6">Gate: the owner's go in UAC-r2's session, recorded. Then import SpaarkeMaster to dev via dataverse-deploy, read back IsSecured and the profile rows.</step>
    <step order="7">Live check (044(e), AC6) on dev; record ids so the owner can delete the test rows (prefix FMSWP-044).</step>
    <step order="8">After merge: TASK-INDEX `✅ [done]`; run scripts/check-task-status-drift.ps1.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If UAC-r2 or ontology-r1 does not agree to the profile design, or wants a different profile or membership, STOP and escalate to the owner with both positions.</trigger>
    <trigger>If any reader of the pair (BFF read path, Worklist, form, view, the external SPA) would lose Read under the design, STOP and escalate.</trigger>
    <trigger>If a server writer of the pair runs under an identity in neither writer profile, STOP: membership is UAC-r2's to change.</trigger>
    <trigger>If securing the columns would change any existing refusal on a path that still creates these tables, STOP and escalate (NFR-07).</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>The #1616 BFF-managed pair (SecureRootInheritance.cs:484, OwnedChildWrite.cs:365, CallerWriteAuthorization.cs:92-97) and its agreement test (RegardingPairFieldSecurityScriptAgreementTests.cs) do this for a project's regarding pair.</existing>
    <extension>Yes: the exemption set is extended with the core pair of two tables, and the profiles gain rows; the new agreement test copies the #1616 test for different columns, because that test is keyed to the regarding pair.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>The owner decision "the core pair is never half-written, on any path" needs an owner for the paths outside the server, and column security is that owner; after D-204 only the writers create these rows, and column security keeps any other path from setting the pair.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <outputs>
    <output type="docs">The profile design and the Grep proof (notes/task-044-notes.md), sent to UAC-r2 before any import</output>
    <output type="code">The three solution XML files and CallerWriteAuthorization.cs</output>
    <output type="test">tests/integration/data-mutation/Signals/CorePairFieldSecurityAgreementTests.cs and the 042c chat-create tests extended</output>
    <output type="notes">projects/field-mapping-server-write-path-r1/notes/task-044-notes.md (UAC-r2 and ontology-r1 answers, the owner's go, the import and live-check evidence)</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">AC1: the profile design is in the notes as a table: the four columns; Create and Update only for the BFF application identities, the ontology writer application identity and System Administrator; Read for everyone who reads today; UAC-r2's review verdict and ontology-r1's agreement are recorded before any import.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">AC2: the Grep proof is in the notes (commands and results) and shows no client screen, wizard or form script writes sprk_corerecordtype or sprk_corerecordid through Xrm.WebApi or the Web API; it went to UAC-r2 with AC1.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">AC3: the agreement test fails when either column on either table is not IsSecured=1, when a writer profile lacks Create or Update on one of them, when the readers profile lacks Read on one of them, or when the code's exemption set and the secured columns differ (each a seeded drift); it passes on the committed files.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">AC4 (DROPPED by D-204; the general create refuses these tables, 042c fix round 4): a chat create of a Signal and of a Decision Record that sends a whole core pair, the caller holding AppendTo on the core, gives 042c's outcome (created, or 042c's refusals) with the columns secured; one that sends a type without an id gets 042c's fill-or-clear with its warning; step 3 still refuses every other field-secured column with its existing words.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">AC5 (must-not-fire): every other table's step-3 answer is unchanged (existing suites green with unchanged assertions); a project's #1616 pair is unchanged.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">AC6 (live, after the gate): on dev, the four columns read back IsSecured; a Signal and a Decision Record written by their writers carry both columns; a chat create with a core pair succeeds; a user holding only Spaarke roles cannot set either column through the Web API (Dataverse's field-security answer); an ordinary user still reads both columns on an existing row.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">AC7: 052's background carries the 044(d) line; BFF build 0 errors, 0 warnings; verify-suites.sh and ArchTests pass; publish delta and CVE recorded; UAC-r2's verdict line is in the PR.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Test scope: AC3-AC5 only; anything beyond needs a one-line reason in the notes. One seeding proof: set one column back to IsSecured=0 once and show AC3 fails (recorded, reverted).</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Placement Justification (CLAUDE.md §10): the code part is a modification of CallerWriteAuthorization's existing step 3 in the BFF; the rest is solution XML; no new endpoint, service, package or background work; publish delta ≈ 0, measured against a fresh master build; CVE check run.
044(b) is answered by the exemption (constraint 4). 044(c) is step 1 and the ontology-r1 agreement. 044(d) is the 052 line. 044(e) is AC6.
Release note: the Signal and Decision Record core record columns can be set only by Spaarke's server.
Added 2026-11-04 from owner decision 3 and UAC-r2's conditions.</notes>

  <execution><skill>.claude/skills/task-execute/SKILL.md</skill></execution>
</task>
